    "lens": {
      "mechanics": "When spending financed by money and credit outpaces production, more money competes for each unit of output. Gold pays no interest, so a fall in the return on Treasury bonds after inflation reduces the cost of holding it.",
      "data": "Three forces help explain the overnight move. <a href=\"https://bea.gov/sites/default/files/2026-11/pi1026.pdf\">October core PCE inflation was 3.3% year on year</a>, keeping pressure on policymakers to contain prices. <a href=\"https://home.treasury.gov/resource-center/data-chart-center/interest-rates/TextView?type=daily_treasury_real_yield_curve&amp;field_tdr_date_value_month=202611\">The 10-year real yield fell to 2.73% from 2.79% on 25 Nov</a>, reducing gold’s opportunity cost. <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/T10YIE\">Ten-year market inflation compensation held at 2.12%</a>. That points to little change in the inflation premium investors priced into longer-term bonds. The early sell-off and later recovery show how inflation news and shifts in rates can weigh on the same market at different points in the session.",
      "unsustainable": "Interest and debt service cannot indefinitely grow faster than the revenue available to pay them. Spending cuts, restructuring, transfers and money creation change who bears that cost.",
      "would_change": "<a href=\"https://www.ecb.europa.eu/press/calendars/weekly/html/index.SG.html\">The ECB publishes its October policy account at 20:30 HKT today</a>. A surprise on the rate outlook could move the euro and dollar during the US Thanksgiving holiday.",
